1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,320
Eight B combines medication assistance, routes and vascular access
with calculation and error management. We start with the legal and
safety decisions, then practise the technical delivery separately.

2
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:23,928
The environment may be noisy and urgent, but every administration
still needs an indication, a verified product, an appropriate route
and a monitoring plan.

3
00:00:23,928 --> 00:00:36,408
School section Two teaches an expanded ten-right framework, while
other Eight B sections return to eight. We will name both accurately
without pretending the count itself guarantees safety.

4
00:00:36,408 --> 00:00:46,656
The original demonstrations below are preparation for supervised
school practice. They do not complete the medication-math gate or a
clinical skill sign-off on your behalf.

5
00:00:46,656 --> 00:01:01,992
The school distinguishes locating a medicine or placing the container
in the patient's hand from personally delivering the dose. Injecting a
drug, applying a patch or spraying it under the tongue is
administration, not automatically mere assistance.

6
00:01:01,992 --> 00:01:12,072
Before acting, describe the actual physical action. Then identify the
authority for that action, rather than choosing the least restrictive
label afterwards.

7
00:01:12,072 --> 00:01:21,312
A patient's own prescription can support certain assistance
conditions, but it does not give a student permission to inject
anything prescribed to anyone.

8
00:01:21,312 --> 00:01:31,008
Administration may have a separate current clinical pathway and
authorization. Keep that pathway separate from the narrower patient-
request assistance mechanism.

9
00:01:31,008 --> 00:01:43,464
Schedule Two's assistance provision applies through the relevant
category inheritance. When the medicine requires a prescription, it
must already be prescribed to this patient by the appropriate health
professional.

10
00:01:43,464 --> 00:01:57,408
The patient must request the assistance, it must relate to the
emergency service being provided and the medicine must be administered
as prescribed. A container belonging to a relative does not satisfy
those requirements.

11
00:01:57,408 --> 00:02:08,496
The provision also excludes transport between healthcare facilities.
The school's four-precondition banner omits that fifth condition,
although a later school paragraph includes it.

12
00:02:08,496 --> 00:02:21,528
These conditions do not create unrestricted administration authority.
Interfacility medication care and emergency treatment of an incapable
patient require their applicable separate basis.

13
00:02:21,528 --> 00:02:34,632
The school banner includes a request from an authorized family
caregiver. The statutory assistance wording specifically refers to the
patient's request. Do not silently replace it with any family request.

14
00:02:34,632 --> 00:02:48,696
If the patient cannot request or decide, assess the applicable
emergency, substitute-decision or other treatment authority. Do not
call emergency treatment a patient-request assistance event that never
occurred.

15
00:02:48,696 --> 00:03:02,328
The school four-element capacity teaching asks about understanding,
appreciation, reasoning and communication. It supports a discussion
but is not quoted as four verbatim criteria of the BC adult statute.

16
00:03:02,328 --> 00:03:14,712
Use appropriate communication, assess the particular decision and do
not equate refusal or intoxication alone with incapability. The Health
Law lecture explains the legal distinctions in full.

17
00:03:14,712 --> 00:03:26,952
The ten-right list contains patient, medication, dose, route, time and
documentation, then reason, the right to refuse, expiry and integrity,
and response.

18
00:03:26,952 --> 00:03:41,520
Match identity using appropriate available identifiers rather than
assuming that whoever holds the bottle is its owner. An unidentified
emergency patient still needs a safe identification process, not
invented demographics.

19
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:55,032
Check product-specific appearance. The school's clear and colourless
wording is not universal for every legitimate formulation; compare
with the actual manufacturer specification and stop for an unexplained
change.

20
00:03:55,032 --> 00:04:05,448
Right response means a real comparison after care. Ask what changed,
what should have changed and what got worse, then use the answers to
revise the plan.

21
00:04:05,448 --> 00:04:17,904
Build a medication picture from the patient, available labels, records
and reliable collateral. Distinguish prescribed from actually taken;
an old bottle does not prove current use.

22
00:04:17,904 --> 00:04:29,352
Ask about the last actual dose and recent changes, including as-needed
medicines and duplicate ingredients. Document uncertainty rather than
filling a guessed schedule.

23
00:04:29,352 --> 00:04:43,728
Ask what happened with a reported allergy. Nausea, isolated itch and
prior airway swelling require different descriptions, but do not
dismiss the patient's concern or fail to assess a current severe
reaction.

24
00:04:43,728 --> 00:04:54,792
Explain the intended help and assess whether the patient can safely
use the product and device. The history, consent and product checks
all support the same treatment decision.

25
00:04:54,792 --> 00:05:09,480
The school uses adrenal crisis to show why finding a patient's
emergency kit matters. Relevant history and an acutely unwell patient
require assessment of airway, perfusion and glucose, not a drug-only
response.

26
00:05:09,480 --> 00:05:23,760
Current E zero two limits EMR assistance to physically collecting the
patient's own hydrocortisone; EMRs must not prepare or administer it.
That is narrower than the school's broad assist-or-administer example.

27
00:05:23,760 --> 00:05:38,688
PCP administration uses the current adrenal-crisis pathway and
requires CliniCall before hydrocortisone. IV is preferred, but an
authorized IM route should not be delayed for failed or difficult
access.

28
00:05:38,688 --> 00:05:52,224
The guideline emphasizes treatment before movement in suspected crisis
and not walking the patient. If capacity is impaired, identify the
actual treatment authority rather than inventing a patient request.

29
00:05:52,224 --> 00:06:06,864
A palliative patient may have a specific breakthrough-symptom plan and
medicines at home. Verify the current plan, patient identity, dose,
route, timing and supplies rather than assuming the family knows a
universally valid dose.

30
00:06:06,864 --> 00:06:19,920
A MOST document describes treatment orders or scope; it is not
automatically the prescription for every medicine. No CPR is also not
an instruction to withhold all symptom relief.

31
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:31,776
Opioid care still needs the required training, endorsement and
clinical consultation or palliative-team plan. Calling it assistance
does not authorize an otherwise unpermitted injection.

32
00:06:31,776 --> 00:06:44,640
Reassess the intended symptom and the relevant airway, breathing,
awareness and circulatory effects in the context of the person's
goals. Document and communicate both comfort and harm.

33
00:06:44,640 --> 00:06:57,456
The school assistance example gives a salbutamol schedule up to ten
puffs. The current public adult monograph instead lists four one-
hundred-microgram sprays with a spacer, repeatable as required.

34
00:06:57,456 --> 00:07:06,984
Pediatric courses use their own weight criteria. Do not transfer a
child's ten-puff course or a classroom example into a universal adult
prescription.

35
00:07:06,984 --> 00:07:22,152
Inspect the inhaler and spacer, verify remaining medication and use
the specific device technique. A metered-dose inhaler usually needs
one actuation at a time, a good seal and an effective coached
inhalation.

36
00:07:22,152 --> 00:07:38,424
Compare air entry, effort, speech, pulse and symptoms after delivery.
Relief does not prove the cause was benign, and inability to inhale
effectively may require ventilation support rather than repeated
ineffective puffs.

37
00:07:38,424 --> 00:07:49,416
First write the unit you want at the end. A drug amount may be
milligrams, a syringe volume millilitres and a gravity rate drops per
minute. These are different questions.

38
00:07:49,416 --> 00:08:00,624
Gather the real weight, dose rule, maximum, product concentration and
delivery time. A formula cannot rescue the wrong input or decide
clinical eligibility.

39
00:08:00,624 --> 00:08:11,976
Cancel units visibly and carry enough precision. In gravity
calculations convert hours to minutes; a pump-rate calculation may
appropriately retain hours instead.

40
00:08:11,976 --> 00:08:24,864
The school instruction to convert kilograms to milligrams must not
mean changing body weight into drug dose without a dose-per-kilogram
factor. Body mass and medication mass have different roles in the
equation.

41
00:08:24,864 --> 00:08:36,744
Convert mass units explicitly: one gram is one thousand milligrams,
and one milligram is one thousand micrograms. An unlabelled decimal is
unsafe communication.

42
00:08:36,744 --> 00:08:49,008
A ratio-labelled solution can be misread. One to one thousand and one
to ten thousand correspond to different concentrations; read and state
the actual mass per volume on the product.

43
00:08:49,008 --> 00:09:01,368
For the same volume, a product ten times as concentrated gives ten
times as much drug. Say the concentration aloud before translating a
dose into a syringe mark.

44
00:09:01,368 --> 00:09:12,456
A six-hour infusion is three hundred sixty minutes, not six minutes.
Ask whether the result is physically plausible and whether the bag
would empty at the intended time.

45
00:09:12,456 --> 00:09:25,368
For a fictional training medicine, the rule is zero point zero six
milligrams per kilogram with a two-milligram cap. A thirty-kilogram
weight gives one point eight milligrams, below the cap.

46
00:09:25,368 --> 00:09:37,392
The label is three milligrams per millilitre. Divide one point eight
by three to obtain zero point six millilitres. The medication amount
and volume must both be clear.

47
00:09:37,392 --> 00:09:49,488
Reverse-check zero point six millilitres times three milligrams per
millilitre. It returns one point eight milligrams. Then check whether
the syringe can measure that volume accurately.

48
00:09:49,488 --> 00:10:04,056
The school also contains midazolam and pediatric dextrose arithmetic
examples. Those can illustrate multiplication, but their named routes,
drugs and clinical conditions are not automatically authorized PCP
treatments.

49
00:10:04,056 --> 00:10:14,424
A fictional fluid order is seven hundred fifty millilitres over five
hours using a twenty-drop-per-millilitre set. Convert five hours to
three hundred minutes.

50
00:10:14,424 --> 00:10:25,656
Multiply seven hundred fifty millilitres by twenty drops per
millilitre and divide by three hundred minutes. Millilitres cancel and
the result is fifty drops per minute.

51
00:10:25,656 --> 00:10:37,824
Fifty drops per minute on that set is two point five millilitres per
minute, or one hundred fifty millilitres per hour. Over five hours it
supplies seven hundred fifty millilitres.

52
00:10:37,824 --> 00:10:50,160
Set and observe actual drops using the taught procedure, then recheck
after movement and against the bag volume. A calculated gravity rate
is not automatically maintained by the clamp.

53
00:10:50,160 --> 00:11:01,056
In the previous problem, dividing by five instead of three hundred
gives three thousand drops per minute. That is sixty times the
intended rate, not a small rounding error.

54
00:11:01,056 --> 00:11:11,304
The visible unit chain exposes the problem: millilitres divided by
hours and multiplied by drops per millilitre gives drops per hour, not
drops per minute.

55
00:11:11,304 --> 00:11:19,560
An independent checker should rebuild the units from the original
order and tubing, not merely glance at the first calculator screen.

56
00:11:19,560 --> 00:11:32,904
If the answer would require an implausible stream or empty the bag far
too quickly, stop and recompute before adjusting the line.
Plausibility is a useful additional layer, not a substitute for
correct math.

57
00:11:32,904 --> 00:11:44,160
With a true sixty-drop-per-millilitre set, the numeric drops-per-
minute value equals the millilitres-per-hour value, because converting
hours to minutes cancels the sixty-drop factor.

58
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:55,632
For ninety millilitres per hour, that set needs ninety drops per
minute. A fifteen-drop set would instead need twenty-two point five
drops per minute before approved whole-drop handling.

59
00:11:55,632 --> 00:12:04,968
Do not memorize the shortcut without reading the tubing package. A set
change can make the same visible drip rate deliver a different fluid
volume.

60
00:12:04,968 --> 00:12:16,488
A pump requires its own device procedure, unit, concentration and
limit checks. The correct number typed into the wrong unit or wrong
channel is still a delivery error.

61
00:12:16,488 --> 00:12:29,376
For multiple medicines or infusions, calculate each order separately
and keep each concentration visible. Copying one line's rate into
another can produce a correct-looking wrong dose.

62
00:12:29,376 --> 00:12:40,464
Separate calculations do not establish chemical compatibility. Verify
the actual medicines, diluent, connection and any required flushing or
separate route.

63
00:12:40,464 --> 00:12:50,184
Trace the prepared product to the actual patient connection before
starting or changing it. An accurate label on the wrong line does not
protect the patient.

64
00:12:50,184 --> 00:13:02,832
Reconcile cumulative drug doses and total fluid, including relevant
flushes. Observe the site, response and running volume rather than
assuming both lines are functioning because both are labelled.

65
00:13:02,832 --> 00:13:16,488
The school proposes a deliberate ten-second pause before preparation
or delivery. Its purpose is to break automatic action and make the
patient, drug, concentration, amount and route explicit.

66
00:13:16,488 --> 00:13:27,984
This is a rehearsal technique, not a legal stopwatch rule. Do not
withhold ventilation or another immediately necessary supportive
action just to complete a silent countdown.

67
00:13:27,984 --> 00:13:39,168
Use crew roles so the check happens efficiently while immediate care
continues. Speak the intended action and require a closed-loop
confirmation instead of vague agreement.

68
00:13:39,168 --> 00:13:52,464
If the product or calculation remains uncertain, hold that medicine,
maintain supportive care and clarify. The safe response to uncertainty
is specific, not paralysis of the entire team.

69
00:13:52,464 --> 00:14:04,872
A latent condition exists before the final bedside action, such as
confusing storage or an ambiguous label. An active failure occurs in
the current action, such as selecting the wrong ampoule.

70
00:14:04,872 --> 00:14:16,032
The Swiss-cheese model shows how holes in several defensive layers can
align. It does not mean layers must fail in one fixed order or that a
careful person can never make an error.

71
00:14:16,032 --> 00:14:29,520
Anchoring can keep a team tied to the first diagnosis or dose.
Confirmation bias may make the checker seek agreement. Availability
can make a recently seen problem dominate the current assessment.

72
00:14:29,520 --> 00:14:43,800
Useful barriers include clear product separation, standard
concentrations, readable labels, independent calculation and a culture
where anyone can interrupt a mismatch. Reporting should lead to a
specific repair.

73
00:14:43,800 --> 00:14:53,136
The preparing clinician states the task without leading with the final
answer. The checker reads the original source rule, weight and actual
container.

74
00:14:53,136 --> 00:15:05,496
Both independently compute the amount, limits, volume and relevant
rate, then compare. Agreement only on the calculator result can miss a
wrong source, weight or concentration.

75
00:15:05,496 --> 00:15:15,816
Resolve differences by returning to the earliest uncertain input. Do
not average two answers or ask the more senior person to choose by
confidence alone.

76
00:15:15,816 --> 00:15:27,648
Finish with the actual syringe or bag, label, patient connection and
expected monitoring. The independent calculation is one defence, not a
replacement for the delivery check.

77
00:15:27,648 --> 00:15:40,584
If a wrong medication, amount, route or rate may have reached the
patient, interrupt the implicated delivery safely and immediately
assess the patient. Do not wait to finish an incident form.

78
00:15:40,584 --> 00:15:52,152
Determine what actually entered, when and by which route. Retain the
relevant label and preparation information while following the
applicable handling and evidence procedure.

79
00:15:52,152 --> 00:16:02,856
Provide indicated supportive care and seek appropriate clinical
guidance. Do not improvise an antidote or attempt to cancel the dose
with an unapproved opposite medicine.

80
00:16:02,856 --> 00:16:15,360
A near miss caught before delivery may require no treatment for
exposure, but still needs containment and learning. Verify the correct
replacement rather than rushing because the first error was caught.

81
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:27,840
The patient record must truthfully show actual care and relevant
consequences, including the error when it affected care. Do not chart
the intended dose as if it were the delivered dose.

82
00:16:27,840 --> 00:16:39,720
Communicate immediately to the receiving and supporting clinicians so
they can monitor and treat the actual exposure. Silence can turn one
error into a duplicate dose or delayed rescue.

83
00:16:39,720 --> 00:16:52,080
Use the applicable safety-reporting system for errors and near misses.
The report supports investigation of contributing conditions; it does
not replace the clinical record or urgent escalation.

84
00:16:52,080 --> 00:17:08,496
Follow the actual disclosure process with factual, non-defensive
language. Do not guarantee blame-free outcomes or legal
confidentiality. Reflection should identify a concrete prevention
change, not merely promise to be more careful.

85
00:17:08,496 --> 00:17:19,488
A learner prepares two millilitres for a fictional four-milligram
dose, assuming two milligrams per millilitre. The actual container
says ten milligrams per millilitre.

86
00:17:19,488 --> 00:17:29,760
The prepared two millilitres contains twenty milligrams, five times
the intended amount. The division was correct for the assumed label,
but the assumption was wrong.

87
00:17:29,760 --> 00:17:44,904
An independent checker reads the actual label before delivery and
stops the syringe. Rebuild from the original inputs; the required
volume from that product is zero point four millilitres if the order
and all other criteria are valid.

88
00:17:44,904 --> 00:17:56,952
This is a near miss, not an administered overdose. Record and report
accordingly, contain the wrong preparation and address the misleading
storage or assumption that enabled it.

89
00:17:56,952 --> 00:18:09,192
The Week Two plan requires the medication-math gating assessment
before progression. This course prepares you with original problems;
it does not open, answer or submit the school assessment.

90
00:18:09,192 --> 00:18:21,024
For each practice answer, show the formula, units, maximum and
reverse-check. If you can only recall the final number, change the
weight or concentration and try again.

91
00:18:21,024 --> 00:18:34,272
In supervised practice, state the ten-right list from the assistance
lesson and explain how it relates to the eight-right framework.
Include the actual consent and expiry checks, not just a recital.

92
00:18:34,272 --> 00:18:44,856
Bring unresolved source-version differences to the instructor. Do not
solve a confusing school dose by guessing or by importing a different
protocol without naming the change.
